    {
      "title": "Immutable aToken Address in AaveV3FiatReserve Requires Proxy Upgrade on Aave Migration",
      "description": "The aToken address is set in the AaveV3FiatReserve constructor and stored as an immutable variable. The _allocated() function reads the reserve's aToken balance to determine how much collateral is deployed in Aave. If Aave V3 migrates to a new aToken contract (as happened in Aave V1→V2→V3 migrations), _allocated() would read from the old aToken address, returning 0 and causing the reserve to believe it has no Aave allocation. This would trigger a complete rebalance that tries to re-supply all assets to Aave, while the actual funds remain in the deprecated aToken. A proxy upgrade would be required to update the aToken address.",
      "vulnerability_type": "Configuration / Upgrade Risk",
      "severity": "low",
      "confidence": 0.6,
      "location": "AaveV3FiatReserve.sol:32",
      "file": "packages/emptyset-reserve/contracts/reserve/AaveV3FiatReserve.sol",
      "id": "1890234567890123",
      "reported_by_model": "claude-sonnet-4-6",
      "status": "proposed"
    },
    {
      "title": "Implementation Contracts Missing _disableInitializers() in Constructor",
      "description": "The implementation contracts (behind TransparentUpgradeableProxy) do not call _disableInitializers() in their constructors. This means the implementation contracts themselves can be initialized directly, not just through the proxy. While this does not affect the proxy's state, an attacker could initialize the implementation contract directly, potentially causing confusion or enabling social engineering attacks. OpenZeppelin's best practice is to call _disableInitializers() in implementation contract constructors to prevent direct initialization.",
      "vulnerability_type": "Access Control / Best Practice",
      "severity": "low",
      "confidence": 0.7,
      "location": "All strategy contract constructors",
      "file": "packages/emptyset-reserve/contracts/reserve/ReserveBase.sol",
      "id": "2901345678901234",
      "reported_by_model": "claude-sonnet-4-6",
      "status": "proposed"
    },
    {
      "title": "Aave aToken Rebasing Can Cause Persistent Allocation Overshoot",
      "description": "Aave aTokens rebase continuously as interest accrues, increasing the balance returned by aToken.balanceOf(reserve) over time. The _allocated() function reads this rebasing balance. When the coordinator sets a target allocation percentage, the actual allocation creeps above the target as interest accrues. Every subsequent mint() or redeem() call triggers _allocate() which detects the overshoot and attempts to withdraw the excess from Aave. If the excess is sub-micro-USDC (< 1e12), the zero-amount Aave revert (VULN-079) is triggered. Even without VULN-079, the constant micro-withdrawals impose unnecessary gas costs on all users.",
      "vulnerability_type": "Arithmetic / External Dependency",
      "severity": "low",
      "confidence": 0.62,
      "location": "AaveV3FiatReserve.sol:_allocated()",
      "file": "packages/emptyset-reserve/contracts/reserve/AaveV3FiatReserve.sol",
      "id": "3012456789012345",
      "reported_by_model": "claude-sonnet-4-6",
      "status": "proposed"
    },
    {
      "title": "Compound V3 Stale balanceOf May Understate Assets Between Accruals",
      "description": "CompoundV3FiatReserve._allocated() calls compound.balanceOf(address(this)) to determine deployed collateral. Compound V3 accrues interest lazily — balanceOf may return a slightly stale value between accrual events. This means assets() can transiently understate the true reserve value, causing redeemPrice() to return a value slightly below 1.0 even when the reserve is fully collateralized. External protocols using redeemPrice() as an oracle may incorrectly flag the reserve as undercollateralized. A call to compound.accrueAccount(address(this)) before reading balanceOf would force an accurate balance.",
      "vulnerability_type": "Oracle / Stale Data",
      "severity": "low",
      "confidence": 0.58,
